    "created_at": "2020-05-20T15:29:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-05-20T15:32:13Z",
    "body": "> Don't ever pipe anything unknown from the web into your (root) shell.\r\n\r\nAs much as I agree, Starship targets a broad audience of shell users, beginners and experts alike. If you don't want to run unknown shell code, you can install it with any of the supported package managers, with the prebuilt binaries, or you can build it yourself.\r\n\r\nThe reason we have the one-liner is for those who don't yet know about package managers or run in an environment without any. The installer doesn't escalate to running as root unless necessary, which isn't always the case for `/usr/local/bin`. On macOS, the one-liner works perfectly without the need to escalate, for instance.\r\n\r\n> And besides that, it knows nothing about the current installation, so if you have installed into another bindir (because the default one `/usr/local/bin` isn't writeable without root), you have to give this option for every new invocation.\r\n\r\nAgreed, it's certainly not perfect. The ability to set the bindir is admittedly not documented anywhere outside of the script comments, so it is more of a poweruser feature. I would love to find a way to update the same installation, if possible.\r\n\r\n> You could install to `$HOME/.local/bin`\r\n\r\nAs for installing in another path, like `$HOME/.local/bin`, that path isn't always in `$PATH`, and would require users to modify their shell profile, which is not something we can give a simple explanation for that works for all OSes and shells. We can modify those files ourselves, but that risks making destructive changes to peoples' dotfiles; something I'm not totally comfortable doing.\r\n\r\nIf folks have ideas for different ways Starship can be installed, I'm all ears. I just want to make sure we are able to make it work on all systems while being simple for beginners. 😊",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
